n Branches Out, Barcelona, Spain, pp. 74–81. External Links: cite178†Link†aclanthology.org Cited by: cite157†§6.2 .\nL495:   * [24] X. Liu, H. Yu, H. Zhang, Y. Xu, X. Lei, H. Lai, Y. Gu, H. Ding, K. Men, K. Yang, et al. (2024) AgentBench: evaluating LLMs as agents. In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), External Links: cite179†Link Cited by: cite162†§2.2 .\nL496:   * [25] Y. Liu, D. Iter, Y. Xu, S. Wang, R. Xu, and C. Zhu (2023) G-eval: NLG evaluation using gpt-4 with better human alignment. In Proceedings of the 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, H. Bouamor, J. Pino, and K. Bali (Eds.), Singapore, pp. 2511–2522. External Links: cite180†Link†aclanthology.org , cite181†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite157†§6.2 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNot All Test-Time Compute Is Evidence:Model-Specific Bias in LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1)\nciteturn41237view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1\",\"lineno\":125}); Total lines: 472\nL92: These three principles lead to Dia (D isagreement-triggered I ndependent A rbitration): agreement between the intermediate and final predictions is returned directly, whereas such reasoning-stage disagreement activates a small heterogeneous solver panel for candidate-blind, answer-level arbitration.\nL93: Across seven benchmarks and two base models (Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B; cite74†Yang et al., 2025 ), Dia outperforms full chain-of-thought in 12 of 14 settings, including an improvement from 59.0% to 66.0% on MuSiQue (cite75†Trivedi et al., 2022 ), while activating on only ${\\sim}$17% of examples and using up to 47.2% fewer tokens than a fully-connected multi-agent baseline that invokes every agent on all questions.\nL94: A multi-agent baseline that routes independent agent outputs through a centralized judge underperforms direct answer aggregation, e.g., 60.67% vs. 66.00% on MuSiQue with Qwen3-4B, highlighting the importance of the aggregation protocol.\nL95: Contributions.\nL96: \nL97:   1. ①\nL98: \nL99: Three manifestations of model-specific error bias. We identify systematic error persistence across three stages of same-model computation: repeated sampling can reproduce a shared wrong answer, reasoning can preserve an initially incorrect prediction, and self-evaluation can systematically favor a later and more elaborate reasoning outcome (Section cite7†2 ).\nL100: \nL101:   2. ②\nL102: Disagreement-triggered independent arbitration. To mitigate model-specific error bias, we propose Dia, a framework that detects reasoning instability via reasoning-stage disagreement and resolves it through candidate-blind, answer-level arbitration by heterogeneous external solvers (Section cite11†3 ).\nL103: \nL104:   3. ③\nL105: Comprehensive evaluation. Across seven benchmarks and two base models, Dia improves over full chain-of-thought in 12 of 14 settings while activating on only ${\\sim}$17% of examples and using up to 47.2% fewer tokens than the multi-agent baseline (Section cite18†4 ).\nL106: ## 2 Motivation\nL107: ### 2.1 Generation Bias under Repeated Sampling\nL108: \nL109: Repeated sampling from a reasoning model can produce varied reasoning traces while sharing the same failure pattern. We quantify this effect on questions where majority-vote self-consistency produces an incorrect answer. For each failed question $q$, we denote by $C_{q}$ the fraction of incorrect samples assigned to the most frequent wrong answer.\nL110: \nL111: cite76†Image: Refer to caption L112: Dataset  | Initially Wrong  | Error Retained\nL113: Qwen3-8B\nL114: ---\nL115: MMLU-Pro  | 52.29%  | 26.23%\nL116: MuSR  | 42.00%  | 61.90%\nL117: DetectBench  | 43.53%  | 35.54%\nL118: Qwen3-Next-80B- A3B-Instruct\nL119: ---\nL120: MMLU-Pro  | 41.43%  | 32.41%\nL121: MuSR  | 37.50%  | 58.67%\nL122: DetectBench  | 39.07%  | 34.86%\nL123: Figure 2: Generation bias and initial-answer persistence bias. Left: Wrong-answer concentration under SC@20 failures. Each point denotes a failed question and reports its dominant wrong-answer share $C_{q}$; dashed lines mark the uniform-choice baseline. Right: Initial-answer persistence across base-model scales. Error Retained denotes the fraction of initially incorrect answers for which full CoT returns the same incorrect answer.\nL124: Figure cite77†2 shows the distribution of $C_{q}$ under SC@20 on MMLU-Pro and GPQA-Diamond. Across models and datasets, the wrong answers are more concentrated than the uniform-choice baseline. For many failed questions, all incorrect samples converge on a single answer ($C_{q}=1$).\nL125: Figure cite77†2 shows three patterns. First, when repeated samples from the same model are wrong, they often collapse onto a single answer rather than spread across alternatives. Second, error concentration increases with model scale. Third, concentration is higher in the reasoning-oriented models. Takeaway. Increasing the sampling budget of the same model may repeatedly reinforce an existing error mode rather than introduce independent evidence.\nL126: ### 2.2 Initial-Answer Persistence Bias\nL127: Reasoning is expected to provide additional evidence beyond a model’s initial judgment. However, a model may already exhibit a strong answer preference before any explicit reasoning, and the same preference can remain after full reasoning. To examine this initial-answer persistence, we first ask the model to produce a direct answer without reasoning and ask the same model to solve the same question with full chain-of-thought reasoning.\nL128: We measure whether the final answer retains the initial answer, particularly when the initial answer is incorrect.\nL129: The right panel of Figure cite77†2 shows that full CoT often reproduces the model’s initially incorrect answer rather than independently reassessing the question. Same-error persistence is strongest on MuSR and remains visible across model scales and datasets. Takeaway. Additional reasoning can elaborate an existing error without supplying the independent evidence needed to overturn it.\nL130: ### 2.3 Longer-Reasoning Preference Bias\nL131: A model’s prediction can change as reasoning proceeds. To capture this change, we compare an intermediate-stage prediction, obtained at an intermediate reasoning stage where the model is sufficiently confident, with the final-stage prediction produced after the model completes its reasoning. When the two disagree, which we call a reasoning-stage disagreement, one simple way to resolve the conflict is to let the model select between its own two predictions.\nL132: This choice may not be neutral: the model may systematically favor the final-stage prediction simply because it follows more reasoning. We examine this bias with Qwen3-8B on reasoning-stage disagreements. Candidates are anonymized and their presentation order is balanced.\nL133: Table 1: Self-selection on reasoning-stage disagreements with Qwen3-8B. We report preference for the final-stage prediction, overall accuracy when exactly one candidate is correct, directional accuracy conditioned on which stage is correct, and confidence on incorrect selections. Wrong-choice Conf. is the mean softmax probability assigned to the chosen answer token when the selection is incorrect.\nL134: Dataset  | Final-stage Pref.  | Self-select Acc.$\\uparrow$  | Intermediate$\\checkmark$ / Final$\\times\\uparrow$  | Intermediate$\\times$ / Final$\\checkmark\\uparrow$  | Wrong-choice Conf.$\\downarrow$\nL135: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL136: MuSR  | 97.7%  | 44.1%  | 0.0%  | 93.8%  | 100.0%\nL137: MMLU-Pro  | 95.1%  | 63.4%  | 6.7%  | 96.2%  | 100.0%\nL138: Table cite78†1 reveals a strong directional asymmetry in self-selection. On reasoning-stage disagreements, the model selects the final-stage prediction in more than 95% of cases, and its selections are reliable mainly when that prediction is correct. When the intermediate-stage prediction is correct but the final-stage prediction is incorrect, the model rarely selects the earlier prediction. This preference persists with high confidence even when it leads to an incorrect choice. Takeaway.\nL139: These results reveal a longer-reasoning preference bias: the model tends to trust the later, more elaborate reasoning outcome even when further reasoning has moved away from the correct answer.\nL140: ## 3 Method\nL141: ##### Framework.\nL142: We propose Dia (Disagreement-triggered Independent Arbitration) to resolve disagreements between predictions produced at two reasoning depths (Figure cite79†3 ). Given a question $q$, the base model yields a final-stage prediction after completing its reasoning and, whenever a confidence criterion is met at an intermediate reasoning stage, an intermediate-stage prediction extracted at that point.\nL143: For compact notation, we denote the intermediate-stage and final-stage predictions by $a_{E}(q)$ and $a_{F}(q)$, respectively, and let $m_{E}(q)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ indicate whether an intermediate-stage prediction is available. Dia takes these predictions as fixed inputs; the extraction procedure for $a_{E}(q)$ is described in Section cite19†4.1 .\nL144: cite80†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Overview of Dia. The base model produces an intermediate-stage prediction $a_{E}$ and a final-stage prediction $a_{F}$. Agreement returns the shared answer directly; disagreement activates three heterogeneous solvers that solve the original question independently behind an information firewall. Answer-level arbitration aggregates support for $a_{E}$, $a_{F}$, or a third answer.\nL145: ##### Disagreement-triggered routing.\nL146: \nL147: If no intermediate-stage prediction is available ($m_{E}(q)=0$), we return $a_{F}(q)$. If $a_{E}(q)=a_{F}(q)$, we return the shared answer. External solvers are called only on reasoning-stage disagreement, that is, when $a_{E}(q)$ is available and differs from $a_{F}(q)$. We denote this routing decision by\nL148: \nL149:  | $$z(q)=\\begin{cases}1,&\\text{if }m_{E}(q)=1\\text{ and }a_{E}(q)\\neq a_{F}(q),\\\\\nL150: 0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$  |\nL151: ##### Candidate-blind independent solving.\nL152: \nL153: When $z(q)=1$, we ask three heterogeneous solvers to solve the original question. Each solver receives only the question and its answer options and answers the question independently. No solver sees $a_{E}(q)$, $a_{F}(q)$, the reasoning traces behind them, or another solver’s output. Each valid response is normalized to a task-specific answer representation $v_{k}$.\nL154: ##### Answer-level arbitration.\nL155: \nL156: Let $\\mathcal{V}$ denote the indices of solvers with valid responses. We count their support for $a_{E}$ and $a_{F}$:\nL157: \nL158:  | $$n_{E}=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\mathbf{1}[v_{k}=a_{E}],\\qquad n_{F}=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\mathbf{1}[v_{k}=a_{F}].$$  |\nL159: \nL160: If at least two solvers agree on an answer outside $\\{a_{E},a_{F}\\}$, we return that answer. Otherwise, we return $a_{E}$ when $n_{E}>n_{F}$ and $a_{F}$ when $n_{F}>n_{E}$.\nL161: ### 3.1 Theoretical Analysis\nL162: \nL163: We explain why heterogeneous solvers and candidate-blind solving help through a single quantity, the truth margin. Formal statements and proofs are in Appendix cite49†B .\nL164: ##### Setup.\nL165: \nL166: For a question with gold answer $y^{\\star}$ and a panel $S$ of $n$ solvers with average accuracy $\\bar{p}_{S}$, let $\\kappa_{S}\\in[0,1]$ be the fraction of the panel’s expected wrong votes that fall on its single most popular wrong answer, and define\nL167: \nL168:  | $$\\Delta_{S}=\\bar{p}_{S}-\\kappa_{S}\\,(1-\\bar{p}_{S}).$$  |  | (1)\nL169: $\\Delta_{S}$ is the expected vote share of $y^{\\star}$ minus that of the strongest wrong answer. The definition encodes an asymmetry: correct votes always coincide on $y^{\\star}$, whereas wrong votes defeat the truth only if they also coincide on the same wrong answer.\nL170: ###### Proposition 1 (informal; Proposition cite81†B.1 ).\nL171: \nL172: Under independent voting, plurality is governed by the truth margin $\\Delta_{S}$, the expected vote share of the gold answer relative to the strongest wrong alternative. If $\\Delta_{S}>0$, increasing the number of independent votes concentrates the outcome on the gold answer, exponentially fast in $n$; if $\\Delta_{S}<0$, additional votes instead reinforce the dominant error mode.\nL173: Repeated samples from the same model preserve the answer distribution and therefore the sign of $\\Delta_{S}$: for a homogeneous panel, $\\bar{p}_{S}$ is the model’s own accuracy and $\\kappa_{S}$ is exactly the wrong-answer concentration $C_{q}$ of Section cite8†2.1 , and neither depends on the sampling budget.\n"
